[117.698773, "o", "ctive_users_report.json /tmp/before.json && sh cmd.sh && cmp /tmp/before.json active_users_report.json && echo \"cmd.sh reproduces output\" && yq -r '.args[]' jq_args.yaml && yq -r '.filter' jq_args.yaml — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash cd /app && cp active_users_report.json /tmp/before.json && sh cmd.sh &…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.8-27b — 16 tool call(s) so far\r\nDone. All three deliverables are in place and verified:\r\n\r\n- **`/app/active_users_report.json`** — 346 active users (matches the `status == \"active\"` count in the input), sorted ascending by `username`, 2-space indented. Each object has `user_id`, `username`, `email`, `last_login` as `YYYY-MM-DD` (all 346 verified against the pattern), `role_count`, and `primary_role` (JSON `null` for the 50 users with empty roles).\r\n- **`/app/cmd.sh`** — the exact jq command with redirection; re-running it reproduces the report byte-for-byte.\r\n- **`/app/jq_args.yaml`** — `filter` (the full jq expression) and `args` (`--indent 2` as a single string). I execu"]
[117.69878, "o", "ted the exact reconstruction command from the spec (`jq $(yq -r '.args[]' ...) \"$(yq -r '.filter' ...)\" /app/users.json > /app/active_users_report.json`) and it succeeded, so the YAML round-trips correctly through `yq`.\r\n\r\nThe filter: `[.[] | select(.status == \"active\") | {user_id: .id, username: .username, email: .email, last_login: (.last_login | split(\"T\")[0]), role_count: (.roles | length), primary_role: (.roles[0] // null)}] | sort_by(.username)`.\r\n\r\n[result] role=implementor model=qwen3.8-27b tools=16 surface=47bfe6dff29f\r\n"]
